Because neither event can be judged from the share count alone. Suppose a firm has 1,000,000 shares outstanding and we own 1,000 of them. Then we own one-thousandth of the firm. Now suppose the board issues another 1,000,000 shares at $10 per share. The lecture’s arithmetic is $\begin{aligned}
N &= 1{,}000{,}000, & n &= 1000, & \frac{n}{N} &= \frac{1}{1000}, \\
N' &= 2{,}000{,}000, & n &= 1000, & \frac{n}{N'} &= \frac{1}{2000}.\end{aligned}$ Our ownership fraction has been cut in half. That is dilution. But the firm has also received $1{,}000{,}000 \times \$10 = \$10{,}000{,}000$ in cash. So the right question is not simply whether our fraction fell. The right question is whether the firm sold the new shares at a good enough price and whether the cash will be used well.

