The lecture’s answer is that the aggregate net number is too crude. What Meyers calls 6% equity financing is net equity issuance: $\text{net equity issuance}
=
\text{new shares sold}
-
\text{share repurchases}.$ A low net number can arise even when many firms are issuing shares, provided many others are buying them back.

