Yet the market capitalization was far larger: $\begin{aligned}
\text{Microsoft market cap}
&\approx (8.497\ \text{billion shares})(\$26) \\
&\approx \$221\ \text{billion}.\end{aligned}$ That is more than five times book shareholder equity. Shiller even suggests that Microsoft lends some support to the pecking-order intuition: for a long time it retained cash and paid no dividend, and investors were willing to accept that because they believed in the firm’s opportunities and management.

